1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,526
The service prints its version at start-up.
The build derives that version from Git.

2
00:00:09,566 --> 00:00:14,350
On every developer machine the build
produces a version string that makes sense.

3
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:19,416
In CI, the automated build pipeline, it's
the same commit and the same build script,

4
00:00:19,456 --> 00:00:22,974
and the version is either a
bare fallback or the job fails.

5
00:00:23,314 --> 00:00:26,178
Nothing is wrong with the
repository on the server.

6
00:00:26,218 --> 00:00:30,905
Nothing is wrong with the build script. The
CI checkout has one commit and no tags,

7
00:00:30,945 --> 00:00:35,119
and a version computed from history
can't be computed without the history.

8
00:00:35,459 --> 00:00:41,322
A second question follows it into the
incident channel: "Version 1.2.1 has been

9
00:00:41,362 --> 00:00:45,805
released. Why does main still
call itself 1.2.0-plus-three?"

10
00:00:45,845 --> 00:00:49,092
Keep that one in mind.
It has a tidy answer.

11
00:00:50,932 --> 00:00:54,690
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:59,795
You now know what a tag is, a ref that
isn't expected to move, and why a published

13
00:00:59,835 --> 00:01:03,468
one doesn't move. Today you put
tags to work in three ways.

14
00:01:03,508 --> 00:01:08,199
git describe turns history into a version
string. Semantic Versioning is the

15
00:01:08,239 --> 00:01:13,304
convention most projects put on tag names.
Git itself attaches no meaning to them.

16
00:01:13,344 --> 00:01:17,973
And a release branch is how an old
version receives fixes without receiving

17
00:01:18,013 --> 00:01:18,533
features.

18
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:23,383
All three meet in one production question:
which releases contain this fix?

19
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:27,603
read every part of a git describe string;

20
00:01:27,943 --> 00:01:32,239
explain why describe fails or prints a
different answer in a shallow clone;

21
00:01:32,579 --> 00:01:35,534
state the Semantic Versioning
rules the section gives;

22
00:01:35,874 --> 00:01:40,233
cut a release branch from a tag,
backport a fix, and tag a patch release;

23
00:01:40,573 --> 00:01:43,319
answer "which releases
contain this fix" with tags.

24
00:01:45,159 --> 00:01:50,081
git describe. In one sentence: 🟢 SAFE, git
describe names a commit by the nearest

25
00:01:50,121 --> 00:01:55,228
annotated tag in its history, the number of
commits since that tag, and the commit's

26
00:01:55,268 --> 00:02:00,190
abbreviated ID. An annotated tag is the
kind with its own tag object: a tagger, a

27
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:01,354
date and a message.

28
00:02:01,694 --> 00:02:06,172
Precisely. If a tag points at the
commit, the output is the tag name.

29
00:02:06,512 --> 00:02:12,607
Otherwise Git walks back through history,
finds tagged ancestors, picks the one with

30
00:02:12,647 --> 00:02:18,742
the fewest commits between it and the commit,
and prints tag, hyphen, count, hyphen,

31
00:02:18,782 --> 00:02:23,197
the letter g, and the abbreviated
ID. The g stands for "git".

32
00:02:23,237 --> 00:02:28,968
On screen that's v1.0.0, two commits, and
c053f0d. Lightweight tags are ignored

33
00:02:29,008 --> 00:02:30,647
unless you pass --tags.

34
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:35,895
The whole string is also a valid revision.
A version printed by a running service

35
00:02:35,935 --> 00:02:37,606
can be pasted into git show.

36
00:02:37,946 --> 00:02:44,291
Options you'll use. --dirty appends a mark
when tracked files differ from HEAD, so a

37
00:02:44,331 --> 00:02:48,776
build from a modified working
tree can't pass as a release.

38
00:02:48,816 --> 00:02:53,869
--match and --exclude take globs
and decide which tags may be used.

39
00:02:53,909 --> 00:02:59,570
--abbrev=0 prints only the nearest tag.
--long always prints the full form.

40
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:03,751
--exact-match fails unless
the commit itself is tagged.

41
00:03:03,791 --> 00:03:08,692
--first-parent ignores tags that
arrived through merged branches.

42
00:03:08,732 --> 00:03:15,001
--always falls back to the abbreviated ID.
And --contains asks the other direction:

43
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:17,586
which tag comes after this commit.

44
00:03:17,926 --> 00:03:21,785
It fails in three ways. No tags
at all. Only lightweight tags.

45
00:03:21,825 --> 00:03:26,816
And a shallow clone, a clone that was
given only the newest part of the history.

46
00:03:26,856 --> 00:03:31,722
The third is the common one in CI. A
depth-1 clone has one commit and no tags.

47
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:36,879
Fetching the tag isn't enough, because the
commits between the tag and the tip are

48
00:03:36,919 --> 00:03:39,395
still missing, and Git can't count them.

49
00:03:39,735 --> 00:03:44,695
Semantic Versioning 2.0.0. Git
attaches no meaning to a tag name.

50
00:03:44,735 --> 00:03:47,002
This is the convention on top.

51
00:03:47,342 --> 00:03:52,024
A version is MAJOR, MINOR, PATCH.
Incompatible API changes raise MAJOR.

52
00:03:52,064 --> 00:03:57,411
Backward-compatible additions raise MINOR.
Backward-compatible fixes raise PATCH.

53
00:03:57,451 --> 00:04:02,000
A released version is immutable: its
contents "MUST NOT be modified".

54
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:07,188
Any change is a new version. That's the
rule of the previous video, stated for

55
00:04:07,228 --> 00:04:07,786
packages.

56
00:04:08,126 --> 00:04:12,795
Zero-dot versions are initial
development, where anything may change.

57
00:04:12,835 --> 00:04:18,255
A pre-release is marked by a hyphen and
dot-separated identifiers, and has lower

58
00:04:18,295 --> 00:04:23,646
precedence than the release. Numeric
identifiers compare as numbers, so rc.2 is

59
00:04:23,686 --> 00:04:29,174
before rc.11. Build metadata follows a
plus sign and is ignored when versions are

60
00:04:29,214 --> 00:04:34,566
compared. And the specification's FAQ
says that v1.2.3 is not itself a semantic

61
00:04:34,606 --> 00:04:39,002
version. The v prefix is a common
way to mark a tag as a version.

62
00:04:39,342 --> 00:04:43,367
Three consequences for Git. The
pre-release rule is why you needed

63
00:04:43,407 --> 00:04:48,233
versionsort.suffix when listing tags. The
output of git describe is not a valid

64
00:04:48,273 --> 00:04:53,161
semantic version of a later build, because
by the pre-release rule it would sort

65
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:58,089
before the release it names. Tools that
publish packages translate it into their

66
00:04:58,129 --> 00:05:01,415
ecosystem's format. And SemVer
describes a public API.

67
00:05:01,455 --> 00:05:06,158
For a service that nobody links against,
a date-based scheme carries the same

68
00:05:06,198 --> 00:05:08,067
information with less ceremony.

69
00:05:08,407 --> 00:05:13,632
Release branches, on the Git side. When more
than one version is in use, a tag isn't

70
00:05:13,672 --> 00:05:18,584
enough: the old version needs fixes that
must not bring new features with them.

71
00:05:18,924 --> 00:05:22,898
A release branch is an ordinary branch
that starts at the release tag.

72
00:05:22,938 --> 00:05:26,223
Fixes arrive on it, and each
patch release is a tag on it.

73
00:05:26,563 --> 00:05:29,547
There are two conventions
for the direction of a fix.

74
00:05:29,587 --> 00:05:33,655
The Git project fixes on the oldest
maintained branch and merges upward.

75
00:05:33,695 --> 00:05:38,390
Trunk-based practice fixes on the main line
and cherry-picks to the release branch,

76
00:05:38,430 --> 00:05:43,125
which is what the demonstration does. A
cherry-pick copies one commit's change as a

77
00:05:43,165 --> 00:05:47,176
new commit. The team-workflows part
of the course discusses the choice.

78
00:05:47,516 --> 00:05:53,047
The failure mode to expect: after a backport
by cherry-pick, the fix exists as two

79
00:05:53,087 --> 00:05:58,753
commits with two IDs. On screen, 362b359 on
main and its copy c40960e on the release

80
00:05:58,793 --> 00:06:03,237
branch. No tag on the release branch
contains the original commit.

81
00:06:03,277 --> 00:06:07,314
Questions about content need
the tools that compare patches.

82
00:06:09,154 --> 00:06:14,724
Read a describe string as a set of directions.
"From the signpost named v1.0.0, walk

83
00:06:14,764 --> 00:06:19,866
two commits forward, and you should be
standing at the commit whose ID begins

84
00:06:19,906 --> 00:06:25,343
c053f0d." The signpost is an annotated tag.
The distance is a count along history.

85
00:06:25,383 --> 00:06:29,283
The last part confirms that you
arrived at the right place.

86
00:06:29,623 --> 00:06:34,316
The directions can only be written by someone
who can see the signpost and the road

87
00:06:34,356 --> 00:06:38,992
between it and where they stand. A shallow
clone is a traveller who was dropped at

88
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:42,812
the destination by helicopter: no
signpost in view, no road walked.

89
00:06:43,152 --> 00:06:47,920
And describe only ever looks backward. A
signpost on a side road that you didn't

90
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:52,128
come along, such as a tag on a release
branch, is invisible from main.

91
00:06:52,468 --> 00:06:55,205
Where the model breaks:
"nearest" isn't about dates.

92
00:06:55,245 --> 00:06:58,891
It is the tagged ancestor with the
fewest commits between it and you.

93
00:07:00,731 --> 00:07:05,871
Try it now, on paper. Pause me for thirty
seconds. Draw five commits in a row, put

94
00:07:05,911 --> 00:07:10,987
an annotated tag on the third, and write
your answer: the describe string for the

95
00:07:11,027 --> 00:07:11,366
fifth.

96
00:07:16,206 --> 00:07:22,453
Count the two commits after the tag on the
graph: f37f723 is one, c053f0d is two.

97
00:07:22,793 --> 00:07:25,983
Here is the failure from the
opening as a root-cause box.

98
00:07:26,023 --> 00:07:30,743
Read the mechanism line: describe needs a
tagged ancestor and the commits between it

99
00:07:30,783 --> 00:07:31,253
and HEAD.

100
00:07:33,093 --> 00:07:38,289
"No names found". Then, with a lightweight
tag: "No annotated tags can describe",

101
00:07:38,329 --> 00:07:42,685
and a pointer to --tags. Now an
annotated tag on the current commit.

102
00:07:43,025 --> 00:07:48,854
On the tag, the output is the tag name.
--long shows the full form with a count of

103
00:07:48,894 --> 00:07:53,219
zero. Two commits later, predict
the string. Say it out loud.

104
00:07:53,259 --> 00:07:53,935
I'll wait.

105
00:07:58,775 --> 00:08:06,264
v1.0.0-2-gc053f0d. The count is the size
of the range from the tag to HEAD, and the

106
00:08:06,304 --> 00:08:10,255
last part is the
abbreviated ID after the g.

107
00:08:10,595 --> 00:08:15,009
--exact-match fails on an untagged
commit with exit status 128.

108
00:08:15,049 --> 00:08:18,685
Keep that: it's the check
a release job should make.

109
00:08:19,025 --> 00:08:24,066
A modified tracked file adds "-dirty". After
the file is restored, the mark is gone.

110
00:08:24,106 --> 00:08:28,542
git restore on a path is 🔴 DANGEROUS
in general, because it overwrites the

111
00:08:28,582 --> 00:08:32,474
working-tree file and uncommitted
edits to it can't be recovered.

112
00:08:32,514 --> 00:08:37,494
Here the script discards an edit it made
itself. Twig looks worried, and with a red

113
00:08:37,534 --> 00:08:39,127
label on screen, fairly so.

114
00:08:39,467 --> 00:08:44,081
Which tags may be used? Add a release
candidate one commit back and a lightweight

115
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:44,771
tag on HEAD.

116
00:08:45,111 --> 00:08:48,577
The nearest annotated tag is
now the release candidate.

117
00:08:48,617 --> 00:08:53,613
--exclude skips it. --tags admits the
lightweight tag, and --match restricts to

118
00:08:53,653 --> 00:08:58,969
version-shaped names. Decide these options
once, in the build script, and write them

119
00:08:59,009 --> 00:09:03,877
down: two builds with different options
print different versions for the same

120
00:09:03,917 --> 00:09:04,324
commit.

121
00:09:04,664 --> 00:09:10,662
Now the CI checkout: a clone with --depth
1. Predict what git tag and git describe

122
00:09:10,702 --> 00:09:11,104
print.

123
00:09:11,444 --> 00:09:16,861
One commit, no tags, "No names found".
The tempting fix is to fetch the tag.

124
00:09:16,901 --> 00:09:21,672
Quick quiz, two options. One: with
the tag fetched, describe works.

125
00:09:21,712 --> 00:09:24,329
Two: it still fails. Say it out loud.

126
00:09:33,969 --> 00:09:38,725
Option two. The tag is there, and describe
still fails: "No tags can describe".

127
00:09:38,765 --> 00:09:43,398
The commits between the tag and the tip
are missing, so the tag isn't a known

128
00:09:43,438 --> 00:09:47,890
ancestor and nothing can be counted.
Almost everyone tries this fix first.

129
00:09:52,430 --> 00:09:55,267
With full history, the same
string as on the laptop.

130
00:09:55,607 --> 00:09:59,565
🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick makes a
new commit on the current branch.

131
00:09:59,605 --> 00:10:04,444
The fix was made on main first. Copy it
with -x, which records the source commit in

132
00:10:04,484 --> 00:10:05,149
the message.

133
00:10:05,489 --> 00:10:11,110
The new commit is c40960e, and its message
ends with "cherry picked from commit" and

134
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:16,704
the full ID of the original. Now both lines.
Predict what git describe main prints,

135
00:10:16,744 --> 00:10:20,209
given that 1.2.1 has been
released. Say it out loud.

136
00:10:26,749 --> 00:10:30,367
main describes itself from
v1.2.0, three commits on.

137
00:10:30,407 --> 00:10:36,065
v1.2.1 isn't an ancestor of main, and
describe only looks backward along history.

138
00:10:36,105 --> 00:10:39,864
That's the second question
from the opening, answered.

139
00:10:39,904 --> 00:10:43,944
Now the incident question:
which releases contain the fix?

140
00:10:49,484 --> 00:10:54,528
No tag contains the original fix commit
362b359: the first command prints nothing.

141
00:10:54,568 --> 00:10:59,612
The release has the copy. git cherry compares
patches, not IDs, and its minus line

142
00:10:59,652 --> 00:11:03,704
says that the fix on main has an
equivalent on the release branch.

143
00:11:03,744 --> 00:11:07,176
The plus lines are what the
release branch doesn't have.

144
00:11:07,516 --> 00:11:10,435
When 1.3.0 is cut from main:

145
00:11:17,575 --> 00:11:25,196
main now describes itself as v1.3.0, and
the range from v1.2.1 to v1.3.0 lists three

146
00:11:25,236 --> 00:11:32,126
commits, including the original of the
fix that 1.2.1 already has as a copy.

147
00:11:33,966 --> 00:11:35,615
Five mistakes to watch for.

148
00:11:35,955 --> 00:11:40,781
Computing a version in a shallow CI checkout.
Root cause: describe needs a tagged

149
00:11:40,821 --> 00:11:45,528
ancestor and every commit between it and
HEAD, and a depth-1 clone has neither.

150
00:11:45,868 --> 00:11:50,463
Fetching only the tag to "fix" the shallow
build. Root cause: the commits between

151
00:11:50,503 --> 00:11:54,754
the tag and the tip are still missing,
so the tag is not connected to HEAD.

152
00:11:55,094 --> 00:12:00,135
Tagging releases with lightweight tags
and wondering why describe ignores them.

153
00:12:00,175 --> 00:12:04,252
Root cause: describe uses annotated
tags unless --tags is given.

154
00:12:04,591 --> 00:12:07,909
Looking for the fix's commit
ID on the release branch.

155
00:12:07,949 --> 00:12:13,132
Root cause: a cherry-pick creates a new
commit with a new ID; containment by ID does

156
00:12:13,172 --> 00:12:14,065
not see copies.

157
00:12:14,405 --> 00:12:17,481
Publishing a describe string
as a semantic version.

158
00:12:17,521 --> 00:12:22,554
Root cause: by the pre-release rule a
hyphenated suffix sorts before the release it

159
00:12:22,594 --> 00:12:22,921
names.

160
00:12:24,761 --> 00:12:30,798
Now, out of the lab. A model-serving team
runs 1.2.x for a regulated customer and

161
00:12:30,838 --> 00:12:37,101
1.3.x for everyone else. The customer's
auditor asks: is the token-limit fix in what

162
00:12:37,141 --> 00:12:37,626
we run?

163
00:12:37,966 --> 00:12:42,808
The engineer doesn't search the release
branch for the fix's commit ID, where it

164
00:12:42,848 --> 00:12:47,874
will never be. She runs git cherry -v
release/1.2 main and reads the minus line for

165
00:12:47,914 --> 00:12:53,000
the fix, and she shows the trailer that -x
wrote on the release-branch commit, which

166
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:58,005
names the original. Then git tag --contains
on the release-branch commit names the

167
00:12:58,045 --> 00:13:03,009
patch release. The answer to the auditor
has three parts: the original commit, the

168
00:13:03,049 --> 00:13:06,549
backported commit, and the tag
that contains the backport.

169
00:13:06,889 --> 00:13:11,993
The same team's release job computes its
version with full history and fails when

170
00:13:12,033 --> 00:13:17,009
git describe --exact-match fails, so an
untagged commit can't be published as a

171
00:13:17,049 --> 00:13:17,517
release.

172
00:13:19,357 --> 00:13:26,856
Your turn. Do Lab 13.3, "Describe and
versions", in lab-manual/m13-tags-versions.md.

173
00:13:27,196 --> 00:13:31,906
Before every git describe in the lab, write
the exact string you expect, all three

174
00:13:31,946 --> 00:13:36,773
parts, or the error. In the shallow part,
predict the output after each of the three

175
00:13:36,813 --> 00:13:38,279
fetches before you run it.

176
00:13:38,619 --> 00:13:44,783
The challenge is Exercise 13.6, Level 2,
draw the graph, "A patch release from a

177
00:13:44,823 --> 00:13:49,745
release branch", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

178
00:13:51,585 --> 00:13:53,516
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

179
00:13:58,356 --> 00:14:03,087
A strong answer takes the string apart in
order and says where each part comes from

180
00:14:03,127 --> 00:14:07,340
in the graph, including what the single
letter means and which kind of tag

181
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:12,054
qualifies. It then states what describe
needs in order to produce that string, and

182
00:14:12,094 --> 00:14:16,652
from that derives the CI failure as a
property of the checkout, not of the tool.

183
00:14:16,992 --> 00:14:20,076
It explains why fetching
the tag alone doesn't help.

184
00:14:20,116 --> 00:14:25,123
It names the layer the default belongs to.
And it offers a fix and a guard: what the

185
00:14:25,163 --> 00:14:29,688
job should fetch, and which check makes
a release job fail loudly instead of

186
00:14:29,728 --> 00:14:31,491
publishing a fallback version.

187
00:14:33,331 --> 00:14:36,258
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

188
00:14:36,598 --> 00:14:41,658
A describe string is the nearest annotated
tag, the number of commits since, and "g"

189
00:14:41,698 --> 00:14:44,998
plus the abbreviated ID; it
is itself a valid revision.

190
00:14:45,338 --> 00:14:49,782
Describe needs the tag and the history
between the tag and the commit, so it fails

191
00:14:49,822 --> 00:14:50,821
in a shallow clone.

192
00:14:51,161 --> 00:14:56,232
Semantic Versioning gives meaning to
MAJOR, MINOR and PATCH, makes a released

193
00:14:56,272 --> 00:15:00,281
version immutable, and sorts
pre-releases before the release.

194
00:15:00,621 --> 00:15:05,798
A release branch starts at a tag and receives
fixes; each patch release is a tag on

195
00:15:05,838 --> 00:15:05,987
it.

196
00:15:06,327 --> 00:15:11,329
After a backport, "which releases contain
this fix" is answered by patch comparison

197
00:15:11,369 --> 00:15:14,002
and by the -x trailer,
not by the commit ID.

198
